\title{Elementary Operations and Elementary Matrices}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{This is a special case of the change of basis formula. Let #{\alpha \colon V \to W} be linear, #{(\mathcal{B}, \mathcal{B}')} bases of #{V}, #{(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{C}')} bases of #{W}; we pass from #{[\alpha]_{\mathcal{B},\mathcal{C}}} to #{[\alpha]_{\mathcal{B}',\mathcal{C}'}}.}

\p{When #{V = W}, a linear map #{\alpha \colon V \to V} is called an \em{endomorphism}. Take #{\mathcal{B} = \mathcal{C}}, #{\mathcal{B}' = \mathcal{C}'}, and #{P} the change of basis matrix from #{\mathcal{B}'} to #{\mathcal{B}}. Then}

##{[\alpha]_{\mathcal{B}',\mathcal{B}'} = P^{-1}[\alpha]_{\mathcal{B},\mathcal{B}}P.}

\transclude{LA2O}

\transclude{LA2P}

\transclude{LA2Q}

\ul{
\li{Elementary row operations are defined in an analogous way.}
\li{Elementary operations are invertible.}
\li{These operations can be realised through the action of \em{elementary matrices}.}
}

\transclude{LA2R}

\p{The link between elementary operations and elementary matrices: an elementary column (row) operation can be performed by multiplying #{A} by the corresponding elementary matrix from the right (left). (Check as exercise.) E.g.}

##{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 4 & 3 \end{pmatrix}.}

\p{\strong{Variation 1: Gauss’ pivot algorithm}}

\p{If you use \em{only} row operations, we can reach the so-called \em{row echelon form} of the matrix: each nonzero row begins with a leading #{1} (a \em{pivot}), each pivot lies strictly to the right of the pivot in the row above, and the zero rows come last.}

\p{Algorithm:}

\ul{
\li{Assume that #{a_{i1} \neq 0} for some #{i}.}
\li{Swap rows #{i} and #{1}.}
\li{Divide the first row by #{\lambda = a_{i1}} to get #{1} in position #{(1,1)}.}
\li{Use this #{1} to clear the rest of the first column.}
\li{Move to the 2nd column.}
\li{Iterate.}
}

\p{This procedure is exactly what you do when solving a linear system of equations: Gauss’ pivot algorithm.}

\p{\strong{Variation 2: Representation of square invertible matrices}}

\transclude{LA2S}

\p{Outcome: #{AE_1 \cdots E_c = I_n}, so #{A^{-1} = E_1 \cdots E_c}. This gives an algorithm for computing #{A^{-1}}, and for solving the linear system of equations #{Ax = b}.}

\transclude{LA2T}
